course: everyday
lesson: 356
title: Peeking at presents and secrets
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Two weeks before his birthday, Ethan in Denver found his presents at the top of Mum's wardrobe and peeked. Nobody ever found out. But on the day he had to act surprised, the surprise felt flat, and he felt guilty every time Mum smiled. A month later he told her. She laughed a little and said «Surprises are part of the fun — for both of us», and he decided to wait next time.
explain: Freya read her big sister's diary and found out a secret that made her feel strange and sad. Her sister noticed the diary had been moved and was furious: «That's MINE. That's private!» For a week she would not talk to Freya. Freya said sorry, promised never to read it again, and kept the secret to herself; slowly her sister trusted her again.
explain: Snooping means looking at things that are meant for someone else: diaries, messages, letters, desks, conversations through a door. It can spoil good surprises, scare you with things you half understand, and break trust.
explain: Owen looked in the teacher's desk while she was out; she came back, saw him, and he stayed in at break. Leo glanced at the test answers on her desk, then owned up to the teacher and took the test again. Jake went on his brother's computer while he was out and left a game open; his brother knew at once and stopped trusting him in his room.
explain: If something makes you curious or worried, ask the person openly. Asking is honest; snooping is taking what was not given.
fact: A diary is private; reading someone else's diary without permission breaks their trust.
fact: Letters addressed to someone are for that person; in many countries opening another person's post on purpose is against the law.
fact: Things heard half through a door are often misunderstood and can be more frightening than the truth.
fact: Peeking at presents usually spoils the surprise for you, not only for the giver.
fact: Looking at test answers before a test is cheating, even if you only glanced.
fact: When you are worried about something you saw or heard, telling a trusted adult helps more than keeping it in.
fact: Snooping can hurt the snooper too: you may read something unkind about yourself.
fact: Trust takes a short time to break and a long time to rebuild.
fact: Not being caught does not stop the guilty feeling that often comes with snooping.
exercise: Why did Ethan peek at his presents? | He could not wait to know | Mum asked him to check them | He was looking for his shoes | His brother dared him to do it
exercise: Nobody found out Ethan had peeked. What did it cost him? | The surprise felt flat and he felt guilty | Nothing, nobody ever found out | His presents were taken back | He had to wait another year
exercise: What did Ethan do in the end? | He told Mum and decided to wait next time | He peeked at Mum's presents too | He pretended it never happened and kept quiet | He asked Mum to swap them for different presents
exercise: Freya read her sister's diary. Why was her sister so upset? | A diary is private, and her trust was broken | The diary was very expensive | Freya spilled juice on it | Her sister wanted Freya to read it aloud
exercise: What put things right between Freya and her sister? | Saying sorry, promising not to read it, and keeping the secret | Telling everyone the secret too, so that it is all fair | Writing in the diary to say sorry | Hiding the diary somewhere else
exercise: Kai looked at Mum's phone messages and read something that worried him. What was the better choice? | Ask Mum about it instead of snooping | Read more of them to understand | Tell his friends what he read | Delete the messages
exercise: Aria listened at the door when her parents were talking and heard something that scared her. Why can half-heard talk be so scary? | You hear only part, so you fill gaps with worries | Doors make voices sound louder and angrier than they are | Parents only talk about bad things | It is always worse than the truth
exercise: What helped Aria in the end? | Telling Mum she had listened, so Mum could explain | Listening at the door again | Keeping the worry to herself | Asking her brother to listen for her
exercise: Owen looked in the teacher's desk while she was out. She came back and saw. What was the consequence? | He stayed in at break | He was made class monitor for being so interested | She gave him a sticker | Nothing, desks are for everyone
exercise: Mia opened a letter addressed to Dad. It was a bill and she worried about money. What was wrong? | The letter was for Dad, not for her | Bills are only for mums to open | She opened it with the wrong hand | It was the wrong colour envelope
exercise: What was the best thing Mia did after? | Said sorry and told Dad she was worried | Hid the bill under her bed so Dad would not worry | Paid the bill with her pocket money | Opened the other letters too
exercise: Leo looked at the test answers on the teacher's desk. What did he do to put it right? | Told the teacher and took the test again | Got full marks and said nothing, since he only glanced | Shared the answers fairly with everyone | Blamed the teacher for leaving them out
exercise: Why was Leo's glance at the answers cheating, even though he only glanced? | His mark would not show what he really knew | A quick glance is never cheating | Only copying from a friend counts | It is fine if you are nervous
exercise: Jake looked at his brother's computer while he was out and left a game open. What happened? | His brother knew and stopped trusting him in his room | His brother thanked him | Nobody noticed at all, so it was fine | The computer broke and his brother had to buy a new one
exercise: Ethan's friend says «I peek every year and never get caught». What is true? | Not being caught doesn't stop the spoiled surprise or the guilt | It proves peeking is fine | It means his parents don't mind | It means presents should be hidden better next year
test: found mum's hiding spot for xmas presents lol shall i look. What's the wise choice? | Leave them, so the surprise stays a surprise | Look but put them back | Shake them all one by one to guess what is inside | Open one and wrap it again
test: sis left her diary on the sofa, «go on, just one page». Best move? | Don't read it, it's private | Read only one page, because one page doesn't count | Read it and tell her about it later | Take a photo of the pages for later
test: little bro: «I heard Mum and Dad say we're moving and I'm scared». He was listening at the door. Best advice? | Ask Mum and Dad straight out what's happening | Listen at the door again tonight to find out more | Pack your bag just in case | Tell the whole class
test: «teacher left the answers on her desk, I'm gonna have a quick look». What's right? | Don't look, that's cheating | Look, but don't write any of the answers down | Look and share it with your friends | Only look at the really hard ones
test: Dad: «Who opened my letter?» It was you. Best reply? | «Me, sorry. I was worried what it was» | «It must have come open by itself in the post» | «The dog did it» | «It was already open»
test: peeked at my presents last year and nobody knew, «it worked last time, so it's fine». Is it? | No, it still spoils the surprise and breaks trust | Yes, if nobody knew last time then it's fine again | Yes, presents are yours anyway | Only if you act surprised
test: read my mate's note about me and it said I'm annoying. I'm gutted. What's the lesson? | Snooping can hurt you too | Notes are always true, so read them | You should read more notes | Your mate should hide notes better
test: brother: «My PC's been moved and the game's open. Were you on it?» You were. Best reply? | «Yes, sorry, I'll ask next time» | «No, it was Mum, she was cleaning in there» | «It turns on by itself» | «Prove it»
test: Mum's phone pings and you see half a message: «bad news about…». You want to read the rest. Best move? | Ask Mum if everything is OK | Unlock her phone and read it | Read it later when she's asleep | Tell your friends it's bad news
test: mate says «I read my sister's diary but she'll never know so it's not really wrong». What's true? | It's wrong whether she knows or not | It's only wrong if she finds out you read it | Diaries are for reading | Sisters don't have secrets
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the reading of another person's diary without consent is wrong. | It intrudes upon that person's privacy and breaches trust | Diaries contain no information of value | Only diaries of adults are private | It is wrong only in the case where its contents are later disclosed to others
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises information obtained by listening at a closed door? | It is frequently incomplete and liable to be misunderstood | It is invariably more accurate than any answer which might be given directly | It is the recommended means of learning family news | It cannot cause distress to the listener
exam: It is given that a pupil has viewed the answers to a test before sitting it. Select the response consistent with honesty. | The pupil informs the teacher and sits the test afresh | The pupil retains the mark obtained, since the answers were seen only briefly | The pupil distributes the answers equally | The pupil remains silent unless questioned
exam: State the course of action most consistent with prudence where a child is troubled by something seen in a parent's correspondence. | The child acknowledges the intrusion and asks the parent directly | The child conducts a further and more careful examination of the correspondence | The child confides the matter to classmates | The child conceals the matter indefinitely
